00:18:48,590 --> 00:18:50,112
Do you know what that's called?

527
00:18:50,112 --> 00:18:51,070
AUDIENCE: Leading edge.

528
00:18:51,070 --> 00:18:52,150
RANDY GORDON: Leading
edge flaps, right.

529
00:18:52,150 --> 00:18:52,780
OK, perfect.

530
00:18:52,780 --> 00:18:53,710
All right.

531
00:18:53,710 --> 00:18:54,726
What's that called?

532
00:18:54,726 --> 00:18:55,620
AUDIENCE: Ailerons.

533
00:18:55,620 --> 00:18:57,993
RANDY GORDON: Ailerons.

534
00:18:57,993 --> 00:18:58,660
What did I hear?

535
00:18:58,660 --> 00:18:59,680
I heard something else.

536
00:18:59,680 --> 00:19:00,580
AUDIENCE: Flaperon.

537
00:19:00,580 --> 00:19:01,288
RANDY GORDON: Ah.

538
00:19:01,288 --> 00:19:02,570
Who said that?

539
00:19:02,570 --> 00:19:03,070
OK.

540
00:19:03,070 --> 00:19:04,254
Explain.

541
00:19:04,254 --> 00:19:07,896
AUDIENCE: It's a combination
of both flap and aileron.

542
00:19:07,896 --> 00:19:10,940
Flaps are on the outside,
ailerons are on the inside.

543
00:19:10,940 --> 00:19:12,098
And-- no.

544
00:19:12,098 --> 00:19:13,140
Did I get that backwards?

545
00:19:13,140 --> 00:19:14,230
RANDY GORDON: Yep,
other way around.

546
00:19:14,230 --> 00:19:15,355
AUDIENCE: Other way around.

547
00:19:15,355 --> 00:19:18,130
And depending on which
[INAUDIBLE] of flight you're

548
00:19:18,130 --> 00:19:21,100
in, you either have full use
of those control surfaces

549
00:19:21,100 --> 00:19:23,470
or part of it is dedicated
to the flap and part of it

550
00:19:23,470 --> 00:19:25,450
is dedicated to
being the aileron.

551
00:19:25,450 --> 00:19:27,040
RANDY GORDON: Yeah.

552
00:19:27,040 --> 00:19:27,540
Outstanding.

553
00:19:27,540 --> 00:19:28,510
That's good.

554
00:19:28,510 --> 00:19:30,700
And this airplane--
this airplane has what?

555
00:19:30,700 --> 00:19:32,020
Ailerons, right.

556
00:19:32,020 --> 00:19:34,750
So if I want to go right
and I move the yoke right,

557
00:19:34,750 --> 00:19:37,693
what happens to the ailerons
here in this airplane?

558
00:19:37,693 --> 00:19:38,860
Sorry, I'll put it that way.

559
00:19:38,860 --> 00:19:40,027
That way, you're setting up.

560
00:19:40,027 --> 00:19:40,970
So I'm going this way.

561
00:19:40,970 --> 00:19:42,610
So this aileron is doing what?

562
00:19:42,610 --> 00:19:43,360
AUDIENCE: It's going that way.

563
00:19:43,360 --> 00:19:44,140
RANDY GORDON: Going
that way, right.

564
00:19:44,140 --> 00:19:45,280
This aileron is doing what?

565
00:19:45,280 --> 00:19:45,850
AUDIENCE: Up.

566
00:19:45,850 --> 00:19:48,490
RANDY GORDON: Up, right.

567
00:19:48,490 --> 00:19:50,170
So I'll cut to the chase.

568
00:19:50,170 --> 00:19:52,270
These are ailerons, these are--

569
00:19:52,270 --> 00:19:54,530
so these are the flaperons
here on the inside.

570
00:19:54,530 --> 00:19:56,830
These are the ailerons.

571
00:19:56,830 --> 00:20:00,560
In a Raptor, those aileron
deflect differentially.

572
00:20:00,560 --> 00:20:02,140
So just like you
have in a Cessna,

573
00:20:02,140 --> 00:20:04,750
they can also both
deflect up, they

574
00:20:04,750 --> 00:20:07,300
can also both deflect down.

575
00:20:07,300 --> 00:20:11,500
The flaps on the inboard are
flaps and ailerons-- flaperons.

576
00:20:11,500 --> 00:20:14,560
So they can deflect up
and they can deflect down.

577
00:20:14,560 --> 00:20:17,090
We'll show why that
brings up some neat stuff.

578
00:20:17,090 --> 00:20:17,860
OK.

579
00:20:17,860 --> 00:20:20,680
Rudder, obviously back here.

580
00:20:20,680 --> 00:20:23,170
In this airplane, when
I push the rudder left,

581
00:20:23,170 --> 00:20:25,690
the rudder deflects
left, I deflect right.

582
00:20:25,690 --> 00:20:27,430
This has two rudders.

583
00:20:27,430 --> 00:20:29,050
Both rudders can
deflect one way,

584
00:20:29,050 --> 00:20:30,370
they can deflect the other way.

585
00:20:30,370 --> 00:20:32,770
They can both deflect in,
and there's some reasons

586
00:20:32,770 --> 00:20:34,060
why you'd want to do that.

587
00:20:34,060 --> 00:20:37,240
And they can both deflect out.

588
00:20:37,240 --> 00:20:39,800
The last piece I'll
give you is back here,

589
00:20:39,800 --> 00:20:41,590
normally this would
be called an elevator,

590
00:20:41,590 --> 00:20:43,540
but on a supersonic
airplane we call

591
00:20:43,540 --> 00:20:46,170
these horizontal stabilizers.

592
00:20:46,170 --> 00:20:48,880
If you guys have read the
story of the Bell X-1,

593
00:20:48,880 --> 00:20:51,313
so Chuck Yeager when
he went supersonic--

594
00:20:51,313 --> 00:20:53,730
we talked a little bit earlier
about supersonic shockwaves

595
00:20:53,730 --> 00:20:56,500
forming on the airplane
as you start going fast.

596
00:20:56,500 --> 00:20:59,200
What Chuck Yeager discovered
is that the elevator,

597
00:20:59,200 --> 00:21:03,280
like in a traditional Cessna,
that horizontal stabilizer

598
00:21:03,280 --> 00:21:04,480
does not move.

599
00:21:04,480 --> 00:21:06,970
So the elevator
at the back moves,

600
00:21:06,970 --> 00:21:10,050
but this part stays fixed.

601
00:21:10,050 --> 00:21:12,550
That shockwave that
would form right here

602
00:21:12,550 --> 00:21:15,940
would actually blank the
air back to the elevator,

603
00:21:15,940 --> 00:21:17,648
and they wouldn't have
any pitch control.

604
00:21:17,648 --> 00:21:19,815
So when they talk about
this thing called Mach tuck,

605
00:21:19,815 --> 00:21:21,340
which was a scary,
scary thing that

606
00:21:21,340 --> 00:21:23,980
happened to a lot of World
War II fighter pilots when

607
00:21:23,980 --> 00:21:25,503
they got into a dive--

608
00:21:25,503 --> 00:21:27,670
they would start forming
shockwaves on the airplane.

609
00:21:27,670 --> 00:21:29,920
They didn't have the elevator
authority to pull up,

610
00:21:29,920 --> 00:21:31,810
and so they would actually
nose dive all the way in

611
00:21:31,810 --> 00:21:34,227
because they go faster and
faster and faster heading down.

612
00:21:34,227 --> 00:21:37,180
The answer was rather
than the back end

613
00:21:37,180 --> 00:21:41,170
of this thing maneuvering,
the entire surface moves.

614
00:21:41,170 --> 00:21:43,005
So as a fighter
pilot, especially

615
00:21:43,005 --> 00:21:45,130
when you got people walking
underneath the airplane

616
00:21:45,130 --> 00:21:47,500
because they're maintainers,
they're working on things,

617
00:21:47,500 --> 00:21:49,880
you always, always, always,
you show them your hands.

618
00:21:49,880 --> 00:21:51,993
Because if you tap
that stick, again,

619
00:21:51,993 --> 00:21:54,160
the stick is not directly
connected just like it is,

620
00:21:54,160 --> 00:21:55,797
the stick's connected
to a computer.

621
00:21:55,797 --> 00:21:57,880
The computer votes and it
allows things to happen.

622
00:21:57,880 --> 00:22:01,333
It then commands a 4,000
hydraulic psi system to deflect

623
00:22:01,333 --> 00:22:02,500
basically this big old wing.

624
00:22:02,500 --> 00:22:04,495
You can actually
take a guy's head off

625
00:22:04,495 --> 00:22:05,870
with the flight
control surfaces.

626
00:22:05,870 --> 00:22:07,270
So you always, always
show your hands

627
00:22:07,270 --> 00:22:09,603
whenever you have people
walking underneath the airplane

628
00:22:09,603 --> 00:22:10,360
for that.

629
00:22:10,360 --> 00:22:12,880
So it's got a very, very
advanced flight control

630
00:22:12,880 --> 00:22:13,690
system on board.

631
00:22:13,690 --> 00:22:15,440
We'll talk through a
little bit about what

632
00:22:15,440 --> 00:22:17,336
advantages that gives you.

633
00:22:17,336 --> 00:22:20,230
The last piece I'll
give you is the engines

634
00:22:20,230 --> 00:22:23,590
at the back, which
are a thing of beauty.

635
00:22:23,590 --> 00:22:25,270
I was a propulsion
guy, aeronautics

636
00:22:25,270 --> 00:22:27,590
engineer kind of thing.

637
00:22:27,590 --> 00:22:30,250
It's almost like modern
art masterpiece to me.

638
00:22:30,250 --> 00:22:33,160
Those engines have
the ability to call

639
00:22:33,160 --> 00:22:34,690
what's called thrust vectoring.

640
00:22:34,690 --> 00:22:37,690
They'll swing up and
they'll swing down.

641
00:22:37,690 --> 00:22:39,620
Where would you
want to use that?

642
00:22:39,620 --> 00:22:42,520
Why do the engines
have thrust vectoring?

643
00:22:42,520 --> 00:22:44,020
You've answered a lot today.

644
00:22:44,020 --> 00:22:44,470
You're smart, though.

645
00:22:44,470 --> 00:22:45,512
You know what's going on.

646
00:22:45,512 --> 00:22:46,360
Go ahead.

647
00:22:46,360 --> 00:22:47,560
AUDIENCE: Very low speeds.

648
00:22:47,560 --> 00:22:48,200
RANDY GORDON: Very low speeds.

649
00:22:48,200 --> 00:22:49,250
Outstanding, yep.

650
00:22:49,250 --> 00:22:50,770
Where's another regime?

651
00:22:50,770 --> 00:22:54,072
Think of the flight
envelope of the airplane.

652
00:22:54,072 --> 00:22:55,530
AUDIENCE: Yeah,
very high altitude.

653
00:22:55,530 --> 00:22:56,905
RANDY GORDON: Very
high altitude.

654
00:22:56,905 --> 00:22:59,285
So why high altitudes?

655
00:22:59,285 --> 00:23:01,452
AUDIENCE: A little lift
from the wings, or the wings

656
00:23:01,452 --> 00:23:02,638
could be small so--

657
00:23:02,638 --> 00:23:03,680
RANDY GORDON: Yeah, yeah.

658
00:23:03,680 --> 00:23:06,530
Density goes way down as
you go up in altitude.

659
00:23:06,530 --> 00:23:07,460
So if I deflect--

660
00:23:07,460 --> 00:23:09,110
I mean, I have to
get more deflection

661
00:23:09,110 --> 00:23:12,110
to move that air to be able
to move the airplane if I'm

662
00:23:12,110 --> 00:23:14,690
relying just on the
aerosurfaces alone.

663
00:23:14,690 --> 00:23:18,590
The way I get around that is
I put two 35,000 thrust pound

664
00:23:18,590 --> 00:23:20,900
engines, if you will,
on board the airplane.

665
00:23:20,900 --> 00:23:23,660
And just like a fire hose, if I
grab a fire hose and I move it,

666
00:23:23,660 --> 00:23:26,330
you would feel that torque
on your body, the same thing

667
00:23:26,330 --> 00:23:29,570
here with the thrust
vectoring on the back.

668
00:23:29,570 --> 00:23:31,490
So with just the
movement of my hand,

669
00:23:31,490 --> 00:23:34,537
I can deflect 70,000
pounds of thrust way

670
00:23:34,537 --> 00:23:35,870
at the back end of the airplane.

671
00:23:35,870 --> 00:23:39,500
So we talked about center of
gravity being right about here.

672
00:23:39,500 --> 00:23:41,593
So that moment arm
of that thrust force

673
00:23:41,593 --> 00:23:43,760
is all the way back here,
and I can deflect that up.

674
00:23:43,760 --> 00:23:46,940
And at very, very low speeds,
where the flight control

675
00:23:46,940 --> 00:23:48,860
surfaces might
not be doing well,

676
00:23:48,860 --> 00:23:52,970
I can still move the airplane
just by using the thrust alone.

677
00:23:52,970 --> 00:23:54,500
At high altitude,
where I don't have

678
00:23:54,500 --> 00:23:57,200
a lot of density, same thing,
I can maneuver the airplane

679
00:23:57,200 --> 00:23:59,770
just by using the thrust alone.

680
00:23:59,770 --> 00:24:01,010
So where that helps you--

681
00:24:01,010 --> 00:24:03,262
because, again, this
is a dog fighter.

682
00:24:03,262 --> 00:24:05,720
Here's what happens, and you'll
