00:26:22,790 --> 00:26:25,610
Now you can configure
it however you want.

752
00:26:25,610 --> 00:26:29,530
Now, the moment you start
putting that in there--

753
00:26:29,530 --> 00:26:30,470
no, got it.

754
00:26:30,470 --> 00:26:32,053
Yeah, the moment you
start doing stuff

755
00:26:32,053 --> 00:26:35,410
like that, you start creating
a software airplane, which

756
00:26:35,410 --> 00:26:37,600
gives you a lot of
flexibility and a danger.

757
00:26:37,600 --> 00:26:39,600
We'll talk about that one
a little bit later on.

758
00:26:39,600 --> 00:26:44,540
What else do you see
flight controls-wise

759
00:26:44,540 --> 00:26:46,965
since we're talking mainly
about flight controls today?

760
00:26:46,965 --> 00:26:47,840
AUDIENCE: Side stick.

761
00:26:47,840 --> 00:26:49,570
RANDY GORDON: Side stick, yeah.

762
00:26:49,570 --> 00:26:56,110
So right there is the
control stick in an F-15.

763
00:26:56,110 --> 00:26:58,330
Sits directly
between your knees.

764
00:26:58,330 --> 00:27:02,390
In a Raptor, that is
your control stick.

765
00:27:02,390 --> 00:27:04,000
It's on the side.

766
00:27:04,000 --> 00:27:05,703
Why would you ever
want to put something

767
00:27:05,703 --> 00:27:06,620
like that on the side?

768
00:27:06,620 --> 00:27:07,120
Go ahead.

769
00:27:07,120 --> 00:27:08,607
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

770
00:27:08,607 --> 00:27:09,940
RANDY GORDON: That's part of it.

771
00:27:09,940 --> 00:27:10,440
That helps.

772
00:27:10,440 --> 00:27:12,107
And there's actually--
you can't see it.

773
00:27:12,107 --> 00:27:14,530
Here it's partly there, but
this little section here, it's

774
00:27:14,530 --> 00:27:16,150
a foldout armrest.

775
00:27:16,150 --> 00:27:18,310
So your wrist is on the stick.

776
00:27:18,310 --> 00:27:21,040
Your elbow, if you will,
it's resting on an arm rest.

777
00:27:21,040 --> 00:27:23,110
So it gives you a lot
of leverage from there.

778
00:27:23,110 --> 00:27:23,890
That's one reason.

779
00:27:23,890 --> 00:27:25,390
What's a couple
other reasons why

780
00:27:25,390 --> 00:27:27,580
you'd want to go side
stick versus center stick?

781
00:27:27,580 --> 00:27:28,960
AUDIENCE: Your legs get in
the way on the center stick.

782
00:27:28,960 --> 00:27:30,377
RANDY GORDON: Legs
get in the way.

783
00:27:30,377 --> 00:27:33,700
That's a big deal
because in this airplane,

784
00:27:33,700 --> 00:27:35,950
you actually got pretty good
after while of flying it,

785
00:27:35,950 --> 00:27:37,500
where if you really need
to maneuver the stick,

786
00:27:37,500 --> 00:27:39,340
you kind of do the leg
up and kind of move

787
00:27:39,340 --> 00:27:41,470
over that way to get
everything out of the way.

788
00:27:41,470 --> 00:27:44,095
The other side about it, too, it
might not be quite so obvious,

789
00:27:44,095 --> 00:27:45,310
but you see how that stick--

790
00:27:45,310 --> 00:27:47,332
that's about where your
eye height would be.

791
00:27:47,332 --> 00:27:48,790
You see how that
stick kind of gets

792
00:27:48,790 --> 00:27:51,730
in the way of seeing
some of the instruments?

793
00:27:51,730 --> 00:27:53,040
It's just sitting right there.

794
00:27:53,040 --> 00:27:55,900
In a side stick, that's
completely off to the side

795
00:27:55,900 --> 00:27:59,590
and all of this real
estate is completely open.

796
00:27:59,590 --> 00:28:02,680
The other thing I'll give you,
again, just for sake of time,

797
00:28:02,680 --> 00:28:05,590
it's a little bit
of a thought change.

798
00:28:05,590 --> 00:28:10,160
When you have a
center stick, it's

799
00:28:10,160 --> 00:28:12,000
like when you're driving a car.

800
00:28:12,000 --> 00:28:14,312
And when you're parking--

801
00:28:14,312 --> 00:28:15,770
you're trying to
park your car, you

802
00:28:15,770 --> 00:28:19,363
want to be able to maneuver
that wheel around a lot.

803
00:28:19,363 --> 00:28:20,780
But when you're
at highway speeds,

804
00:28:20,780 --> 00:28:22,655
if you were to take the
wheel and maneuver it

805
00:28:22,655 --> 00:28:25,280
that much again, you're
going to roll the car.

806
00:28:25,280 --> 00:28:30,950
So in a center stick,
you move the stick around

807
00:28:30,950 --> 00:28:33,210
to be able to deflect the
flight control surfaces.

808
00:28:33,210 --> 00:28:35,210
There's a physical
displacement of the stick

809
00:28:35,210 --> 00:28:36,500
to make that happen.

810
00:28:36,500 --> 00:28:39,830
On a side stick, that stick,
initially when they first

811
00:28:39,830 --> 00:28:43,220
put it in, did not move at all.

812
00:28:43,220 --> 00:28:45,990
So imagine if you were in your
car and you wanted to steer,

813
00:28:45,990 --> 00:28:47,930
and rather than the
wheel actually moving,

814
00:28:47,930 --> 00:28:50,270
you just put pressure
on the wheel itself

815
00:28:50,270 --> 00:28:53,120
and that was enough to transmit
to the computer in the car

816
00:28:53,120 --> 00:28:54,860
to turn the wheels.

817
00:28:54,860 --> 00:28:58,312
That was a problem initially,
which we can talk about

818
00:28:58,312 --> 00:28:59,270
after the presentation.

819
00:28:59,270 --> 00:29:01,130
That's a little bit of
a sidebar discussion.

820
00:29:01,130 --> 00:29:03,020
This is a human factors thing.

821
00:29:03,020 --> 00:29:04,190
It's a side stick.

822
00:29:04,190 --> 00:29:05,510
Initially it didn't move.

823
00:29:05,510 --> 00:29:08,070
Eventually they decided,
I'll put in some deflection.

824
00:29:08,070 --> 00:29:10,730
So I can go a half inch
left, a half inch right,

825
00:29:10,730 --> 00:29:15,347
about a quarter inch forward,
I go about a half inch back

826
00:29:15,347 --> 00:29:17,930
because normally I really want
to get the nose going this way.

827
00:29:17,930 --> 00:29:19,513
If I had that much
deflection forward,

828
00:29:19,513 --> 00:29:21,410
I don't want to fly
the airplane this way.

829
00:29:21,410 --> 00:29:23,175
So you have the
ability to do that.

830
00:29:23,175 --> 00:29:25,550
But it gives you a little bit
of the best of both worlds.

831
00:29:25,550 --> 00:29:27,140
There's some
deflection to give you

832
00:29:27,140 --> 00:29:29,810
feedback that you've input
something on the stick,

833
00:29:29,810 --> 00:29:31,238
but not so much
deflection that it

834
00:29:31,238 --> 00:29:33,530
gets in the way of everything
else you're trying to do.

835
00:29:33,530 --> 00:29:36,470
So there's a little bit of real
estate in the fighter cockpit.

836
00:29:36,470 --> 00:29:38,595
The last piece I'll give
you, and it's a little bit

837
00:29:38,595 --> 00:29:44,960
hard to see here, is all those
buttons there on the stick.

838
00:29:44,960 --> 00:29:46,670
What you have the
ability to do-- so

839
00:29:46,670 --> 00:29:50,540
it's exactly like modern car
steering wheels right now.

840
00:29:50,540 --> 00:29:53,270
You can control volume, radio,
everything else like that,

841
00:29:53,270 --> 00:29:55,010
you can do it from
the steering wheel.

842
00:29:55,010 --> 00:29:56,090
Same thing in a fighter.

843
00:29:56,090 --> 00:29:58,455
It's called hands-on
throttle and stick, HOTAS.

844
00:29:58,455 --> 00:30:00,080
And it allows you to
control everything

845
00:30:00,080 --> 00:30:01,788
that you would want
to do on the airplane

846
00:30:01,788 --> 00:30:04,430
with never having
to leave your hands

847
00:30:04,430 --> 00:30:06,380
from the throttle on the stick.

848
00:30:06,380 --> 00:30:08,540
The other piece I'll give
you is that in a Raptor--

849
00:30:08,540 --> 00:30:11,060
so in a fighter like
this, this is your stick,

850
00:30:11,060 --> 00:30:12,280
this is your throttle.

851
00:30:12,280 --> 00:30:14,480
In a Raptor, because
the flight control modes

852
00:30:14,480 --> 00:30:17,540
can change so much, this
is your right inceptor

853
00:30:17,540 --> 00:30:20,180
and this is your left inceptor.

854
00:30:20,180 --> 00:30:23,130
It's all considered part of
the flight control system.

855
00:30:23,130 --> 00:30:23,630
All right.

856
00:30:23,630 --> 00:30:26,200
Let's keep going.

857
00:30:26,200 --> 00:30:30,690
So just like on your car where
if you want to go to XM radio,

858
00:30:30,690 --> 00:30:33,630
you go to media
source or whatever

859
00:30:33,630 --> 00:30:36,720
and go XM satellite
radio, you have buttons

860
00:30:36,720 --> 00:30:39,990
because it's a software-driven
jet, just like we saw before,

861
00:30:39,990 --> 00:30:42,390
and I can pull up a flight
control display that

862
00:30:42,390 --> 00:30:45,130
shows me the position of all
my flight control surfaces.

863
00:30:45,130 --> 00:30:49,020
Again, the pilot is way
forward on the front end

864
00:30:49,020 --> 00:30:49,740
of this airplane.

865
00:30:49,740 --> 00:30:53,160
As I deflect things, sometimes
I can't actually see them.

866
00:30:53,160 --> 00:30:55,313
And so initially, this
was a flight test display

867
00:30:55,313 --> 00:30:57,480
so that I don't have to do
the old poltergeist thing

868
00:30:57,480 --> 00:30:58,980
and try to rotate
all the way around

869
00:30:58,980 --> 00:31:01,140
to be able to see to the
back end of the airplane

870
00:31:01,140 --> 00:31:02,940
to see if the elevators moving.

871
00:31:02,940 --> 00:31:04,860
I basically instrument
everything and I

872
00:31:04,860 --> 00:31:05,880
can see it here.

873
00:31:05,880 --> 00:31:07,590
Now, some of the
human factor stuff

874
00:31:07,590 --> 00:31:09,510
that's kind of cool
with how they did this--

875
00:31:12,950 --> 00:31:15,730
so again, this tells you, at
the top, rudder deflection

876
00:31:15,730 --> 00:31:19,270
surfaces, ailerons, where the
horizontal stabilizers are at,

877
00:31:19,270 --> 00:31:20,680
how the engines are doing.

878
00:31:20,680 --> 00:31:22,070
This is a little weird.

879
00:31:22,070 --> 00:31:23,380
I don't really like
how they did this one,

880
00:31:23,380 --> 00:31:25,750
but this is the leading edge
flaps, LE flaps, the leading

881
00:31:25,750 --> 00:31:26,180
edge flaps.

882
00:31:26,180 --> 00:31:28,472
So it just shows you how far
they're deflected, dug in,

883
00:31:28,472 --> 00:31:30,100
dug down.

884
00:31:30,100 --> 00:31:33,680
On the lower left-hand corner,
it shows you center of gravity.

885
00:31:33,680 --> 00:31:35,380
So there's limits.

886
00:31:35,380 --> 00:31:36,910
Just like what
you would normally

887
00:31:36,910 --> 00:31:38,670
do on flight planning
for your Cessna--

888
00:31:38,670 --> 00:31:41,020
hey, I can only put this much
fuel with this much passengers,

889
00:31:41,020 --> 00:31:42,760
and I've got to be within
this limit for takeoff,

890
00:31:42,760 --> 00:31:45,430
for landing and everything else,
the airplane tells you that.

891
00:31:45,430 --> 00:31:47,472
Now, what's cool about
some of the things they've

892
00:31:47,472 --> 00:31:49,510
done in Raptor is that
if it's out of limits,

893
00:31:49,510 --> 00:31:51,490
again, it doesn't expect you
to get out the piece of paper

894
00:31:51,490 --> 00:31:53,050
with the whiz wheel and the E6B.

895
00:31:53,050 --> 00:31:54,550
It just changes color.

896
00:31:54,550 --> 00:31:58,840
It goes red and it gives you a
warning that says, hey, idiot,

897
00:31:58,840 --> 00:32:01,952
you are beyond the
aft center of gravity.

898
00:32:01,952 --> 00:32:03,910
No gauge tells you it's
just having a hard time

899
00:32:03,910 --> 00:32:05,380
seeing where all the fuel
is and everything else.

900
00:32:05,380 --> 00:32:06,530
That's a different story.

901
00:32:06,530 --> 00:32:10,270
But it tells you what percentage
of mean aerodynamic chord

902
00:32:10,270 --> 00:32:13,370
that you're sitting at and it's
able to adjust stuff around.

903
00:32:13,370 --> 00:32:16,570
So I'll give that to you
because what this allows is--

904
00:32:16,570 --> 00:32:18,820
oh, by the way, the last
part here is flight control--

905
00:32:18,820 --> 00:32:21,340
so in other words, this tells
you where you moved your stick

906
00:32:21,340 --> 00:32:22,473
and it tells you trim.

907
00:32:22,473 --> 00:32:23,890
So you know where
the trim is set,

908
00:32:23,890 --> 00:32:25,765
and it also tells you
where the stick is set.

909
00:32:25,765 --> 00:32:27,910
So you know your inputs
that go through there.

910
00:32:27,910 --> 00:32:30,220
When you have a
software-driven machine,

911
00:32:30,220 --> 00:32:34,220
you can customize things
and make it very simple.

912
00:32:34,220 --> 00:32:36,760
You guys have seen that
terrible, terrible awful movie

913
00:32:36,760 --> 00:32:37,700
Top Gun, right?

914
00:32:37,700 --> 00:32:39,190
It's just awful, right?

915
00:32:39,190 --> 00:32:41,965
So that scene where
Maverick loses his engines

916
00:32:41,965 --> 00:32:44,590
and they go through this, engine
one is out, engine two is out,

917
00:32:44,590 --> 00:32:47,950
and they go on this flat spin
to sea, which is a bunch of BS.

918
00:32:47,950 --> 00:32:50,710
When you're in a spin, you
don't translate over the ground,

919
00:32:50,710 --> 00:32:51,820
you fall straight down.

920
00:32:51,820 --> 00:32:54,205
So this whole flying out
to sea thing is ridiculous.

921
00:32:54,205 --> 00:32:55,330
It doesn't happen that way.

922
00:32:57,985 --> 00:32:59,860
If you don't have a
software-driven airplane,

923
00:32:59,860 --> 00:33:01,750
when you lose engines like that,
then you've got to go through

924
00:33:01,750 --> 00:33:02,560
this whole--

925
00:33:02,560 --> 00:33:06,413
for instance, in the Cessna,
if you lose the motor,

926
00:33:06,413 --> 00:33:07,330
what do you got to do?

927
00:33:07,330 --> 00:33:09,710
Establish your glide, get
everything set up, check to see

928
00:33:09,710 --> 00:33:11,710
am I my left, right, or
both on the fuel source,

929
00:33:11,710 --> 00:33:14,410
what's my whatever, blah, blah,
blah, and everything else.

930
00:33:14,410 --> 00:33:16,660
In the Raptor it's the most
awesome thing possible,

931
00:33:16,660 --> 00:33:18,760
you literally do nothing.

932
00:33:18,760 --> 00:33:21,530
You sit there and the computer
goes, oh, you've lost a motor.

933
00:33:21,530 --> 00:33:23,168
I've noticed that
you have a problem.

934
00:33:23,168 --> 00:33:25,210
Let me see if I can help
you, and it will restart

935
00:33:25,210 --> 00:33:26,750
the motor for you if it can.

936
00:33:26,750 --> 00:33:28,608
If it can't restart
the motor, chances

937
00:33:28,608 --> 00:33:31,150
are it ain't going to restart
and you can leave it shut down,

938
00:33:31,150 --> 00:33:32,290
if you will.

939
00:33:32,290 --> 00:33:34,900
It allows some neat
human factor designs

940
00:33:34,900 --> 00:33:37,708
because in this airplane-- in
the Raptor, believe it or not,

941
00:33:37,708 --> 00:33:39,250
when you're flying
the Raptor, you're

942
00:33:39,250 --> 00:33:41,950
not thinking about
flying the Raptor.

943
00:33:41,950 --> 00:33:43,822
You're thinking about
employing the Raptor.

944
00:33:43,822 --> 00:33:46,030
So you're trying to find
where all of your wing mates

945
00:33:46,030 --> 00:33:49,180
are, where the bad guys
are, et cetera, et cetera.

946
00:33:49,180 --> 00:33:51,730
Flying is secondary.

947
00:33:51,730 --> 00:33:54,520
Whereas in this plane,
flying is everything.

948
00:33:54,520 --> 00:33:56,920
So they tried to
as much as possible

949
00:33:56,920 --> 00:33:59,740
alleviate the pilot from as
much burden of responsibility

950
00:33:59,740 --> 00:34:01,750
of thinking about flying
the airplane by doing

951
00:34:01,750 --> 00:34:03,650
neat little tricks like that.

952
00:34:03,650 --> 00:34:06,108
The other thing I'll give you
too about the flight controls

953
00:34:06,108 --> 00:34:08,830
is, again, we talked about
how ailerons can go up,

954
00:34:08,830 --> 00:34:11,590
ailerons can come down,
flaperons go up, they go down,

955
00:34:11,590 --> 00:34:13,030
rudders come out and go down.

956
00:34:13,030 --> 00:34:16,880
On takeoff mode, I'll show
you something kind of cool.

957
00:34:16,880 --> 00:34:19,780
So here's the nose look on here.

958
00:34:19,780 --> 00:34:21,760
On takeoff mode,
without any input

959
00:34:21,760 --> 00:34:24,790
from the pilot-- so there's
no flap switch in the cockpit,

960
00:34:24,790 --> 00:34:27,877
there's no leading edge,
there's nothing like that.

961
00:34:27,877 --> 00:34:29,710
It just knows, based
on the fact that you've

962
00:34:29,710 --> 00:34:32,192
got the gear handled down and
that you're on the ground,

963
00:34:32,192 --> 00:34:34,150
that it senses the weight
on wheels, it thinks,

964
00:34:34,150 --> 00:34:35,770
this guy wants to take off.

965
00:34:35,770 --> 00:34:39,219
So the rudders will
both deflect inward.

966
00:34:39,219 --> 00:34:41,070
Why would you do that?

967
00:34:41,070 --> 00:34:41,570
Go ahead.

968
00:34:41,570 --> 00:34:45,449
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

969
00:34:50,550 --> 00:34:53,284
RANDY GORDON: So not quite.

970
00:34:53,284 --> 00:34:55,409
There is a tie between the
flight controls, though.

971
00:34:55,409 --> 00:34:57,076
So in other words,
if I take the rudders

972
00:34:57,076 --> 00:35:00,920
and I tow them both in, so
both rudders are deflected in,

973
00:35:00,920 --> 00:35:03,875
what does that do from an
aerodynamic standpoint?

974
00:35:03,875 --> 00:35:06,500
Which way's the nose going to go
if these rudders are deflected

975
00:35:06,500 --> 00:35:07,147
in?

976
00:35:07,147 --> 00:35:07,689
AUDIENCE: Up.

977
00:35:07,689 --> 00:35:09,230
RANDY GORDON: Up, right.

978
00:35:09,230 --> 00:35:12,050
So it makes it easier to
rotate the airplane on takeoff

979
00:35:12,050 --> 00:35:14,210
because the rudders
are deflecting in.

980
00:35:14,210 --> 00:35:16,460
Both of them go in.

981
00:35:16,460 --> 00:35:18,290
The other part about it too is--

982
00:35:18,290 --> 00:35:19,400
so we talked about this.

983
00:35:19,400 --> 00:35:21,710
You guys ever see a
competition aerobatic,

984
00:35:21,710 --> 00:35:24,190
one of those extra
300 kind of airplanes?

985
00:35:24,190 --> 00:35:26,450
See how the aileron
runs the entire length

986
00:35:26,450 --> 00:35:27,542
of the whole wing?

987
00:35:27,542 --> 00:35:29,750
So the pilot, all they got
to do is just tap the wing

988
00:35:29,750 --> 00:35:32,270
and the airplane is going to
do corkscrews and spirals.

989
00:35:32,270 --> 00:35:34,190
On a Raptor, that's
part of the reason

990
00:35:34,190 --> 00:35:36,650
why the aileron and the
flaperon can move together

991
00:35:36,650 --> 00:35:38,250
as one surface.

992
00:35:38,250 --> 00:35:40,760
And so the leading
edge flaps will dig in.

993
00:35:40,760 --> 00:35:43,640
The trailing aileron and
the trailing edge flap,

994
00:35:43,640 --> 00:35:45,420
that will dig in as well.

995
00:35:45,420 --> 00:35:50,120
So on takeoff, with no
input from the pilot,

996
00:35:50,120 --> 00:35:53,090
whereas the wing normally
would look like this

997
00:35:53,090 --> 00:35:56,330
with a little bit of
camber on it, on takeoff

998
00:35:56,330 --> 00:35:59,250
the wing looks like this.

999
00:35:59,250 --> 00:36:01,750
It's kind of cool like when
you look at from the side.

1000
00:36:01,750 --> 00:36:04,890
So if you're looking at
it from directly side on,

1001
00:36:04,890 --> 00:36:07,650
that leading edge flap
comes down, these come down.

1002
00:36:07,650 --> 00:36:11,040
That puts in a whole bunch
of camber on the wing,

1003
00:36:11,040 --> 00:36:12,720
and it generates a lot of lift.

1004
00:36:12,720 --> 00:36:14,970
And again, that's completely
independent of the pilot.

1005
00:36:14,970 --> 00:36:17,640
The pilot has done nothing
other than just tell it,

1006
00:36:17,640 --> 00:36:21,370
I'm on the ground because
the gear handle is down.

1007
00:36:21,370 --> 00:36:24,528
PROFESSOR: Laz, what would
be a typical rotation speed?

1008
00:36:24,528 --> 00:36:25,320
RANDY GORDON: Yeah.

1009
00:36:25,320 --> 00:36:26,650
So that's a good question.

1010
00:36:26,650 --> 00:36:28,570
What do you guys think?

1011
00:36:28,570 --> 00:36:29,630
So I start on the runway.

1012
00:36:29,630 --> 00:36:31,297
By the way, I need
8,000 feet of runway.

1013
00:36:31,297 --> 00:36:35,000
This whole 2,500 feet is not
for a Raptor, unfortunately.

1014
00:36:35,000 --> 00:36:35,898
8,000 feet of runway.

1015
00:36:35,898 --> 00:36:37,940
But by the time I actually
get ready to pull back

1016
00:36:37,940 --> 00:36:41,150
on that stick to rotate, how
fast do you think it's going?

1017
00:36:45,540 --> 00:36:47,575
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

1018
00:36:47,575 --> 00:36:48,700
RANDY GORDON: Little close.

1019
00:36:48,700 --> 00:36:50,747
150 is the usual--

1020
00:36:50,747 --> 00:36:52,330
so about take off--
which, by the way,

1021
00:36:52,330 --> 00:36:57,220
this airplane is about
34, 35 tons on takeoff,

1022
00:36:57,220 --> 00:37:01,600
compared to 2,500
pounds or whatever.

1023
00:37:01,600 --> 00:37:02,850
So it's a very heavy airplane.

1024
00:37:02,850 --> 00:37:04,350
It's got to get a
lot of speed to be

1025
00:37:04,350 --> 00:37:05,470
able to get that rotation.

1026
00:37:05,470 --> 00:37:08,990
But it's about 150,
160 knots on takeoff,

1027
00:37:08,990 --> 00:37:12,730
which is faster than the never
exceed speed of the Cessna,

1028
00:37:12,730 --> 00:37:13,960
as it goes.

1029
00:37:13,960 --> 00:37:16,425
But yeah, so the flight
controls are there to help you.

1030
00:37:16,425 --> 00:37:17,800
The other thing
I'll show you too

1031
00:37:17,800 --> 00:37:20,380
is there is-- so
sailplane guys--

1032
00:37:20,380 --> 00:37:21,550
where are my sailplane guys?

1033
00:37:21,550 --> 00:37:22,510
Anyone fly sailplanes?

1034
00:37:22,510 --> 00:37:25,630
So on the left in a
sailplane cockpit,

1035
00:37:25,630 --> 00:37:29,050
you typically have a lever for
the speed brakes, spoilers,

1036
00:37:29,050 --> 00:37:31,450
so these big surfaces
that pop up on the wing

1037
00:37:31,450 --> 00:37:33,580
to slow the airplane
down to just glide slopes

1038
00:37:33,580 --> 00:37:34,720
and those sorts of things.

1039
00:37:34,720 --> 00:37:36,220
We talked a little
bit earlier about

1040
00:37:36,220 --> 00:37:37,870
how the electrical
engineer guys want

1041
00:37:37,870 --> 00:37:40,240
this thing to be completely
stealthy and whatever else.

1042
00:37:40,240 --> 00:37:43,060
The last thing you want to do,
based on the discussion we just

1043
00:37:43,060 --> 00:37:45,520
had, is I don't want to
have a big board stick up

1044
00:37:45,520 --> 00:37:47,395
in the middle of this
airplane because that's

1045
00:37:47,395 --> 00:37:49,700
going to make my radar
reflection go very, very large.

1046
00:37:49,700 --> 00:37:51,408
So they did something
kind of remarkable.

1047
00:37:51,408 --> 00:37:54,970
They used the flight
control surfaces themselves

1048
00:37:54,970 --> 00:37:56,650
as the speed brakes.

1049
00:37:56,650 --> 00:37:58,480
So if I need to slow
the airplane down,

1050
00:37:58,480 --> 00:38:00,250
I hit a little switch
on the throttle.

1051
00:38:00,250 --> 00:38:03,640
And immediately what happens
is the leading edge flaps will

1052
00:38:03,640 --> 00:38:08,200
come down a bit, the
ailerons will both go up,

1053
00:38:08,200 --> 00:38:12,190
the rudders will go out,
so they'll both tow out.

1054
00:38:12,190 --> 00:38:14,980
And it's using that to help
slow the airplane down.

1055
00:38:14,980 --> 00:38:19,090
It's actually quite effective,
which is pretty cool.

1056
00:38:19,090 --> 00:38:19,590
All right.

1057
00:38:19,590 --> 00:38:22,110
A couple other things
we'll go through.

1058
00:38:22,110 --> 00:38:24,500
Landing mode.

1059
00:38:24,500 --> 00:38:25,700
Pilot's done nothing.

1060
00:38:25,700 --> 00:38:29,690
Touchdown, throttles are
back, gear handle's down,

1061
00:38:29,690 --> 00:38:31,460
and all of this stuff happens.

1062
00:38:31,460 --> 00:38:34,030
Here, let me get
this zoomed in here.

1063
00:38:34,030 --> 00:38:35,770
So that's the aileron.

1064
00:38:35,770 --> 00:38:36,730
It's up.

1065
00:38:36,730 --> 00:38:37,600
Holy crap.

1066
00:38:37,600 --> 00:38:39,460
The other one's up, too.

1067
00:38:39,460 --> 00:38:41,500
The flaperon goes
up on either side.

1068
00:38:41,500 --> 00:38:43,000
The rudders, you
can see how they're

1069
00:38:43,000 --> 00:38:46,353
both kind of towing
in there a little bit.

1070
00:38:46,353 --> 00:38:47,770
The reason it's
doing that is it's

1071
00:38:47,770 --> 00:38:50,860
trying to transfer all of
that weight from the wings

1072
00:38:50,860 --> 00:38:51,920
and put it into the gear.

1073
00:38:51,920 --> 00:38:54,418
So it's trying to kill all
of the lift on the wings

1074
00:38:54,418 --> 00:38:55,960
and transfer that
to the landing gear

1075
00:38:55,960 --> 00:38:58,780
so that you have the maximum
traction on the wheels

1076
00:38:58,780 --> 00:39:02,640
as you hit the brakes
as you slow down.

1077
00:39:02,640 --> 00:39:08,660
Also, on refueling-- so
when you go air refueling,

1078
00:39:08,660 --> 00:39:11,900
there's a little switch
that you open in the jet.

1079
00:39:11,900 --> 00:39:13,052
That opens up this door.

1080
00:39:13,052 --> 00:39:14,510
That tells the jet
that I'm getting

1081
00:39:14,510 --> 00:39:16,480
ready to refuel
while I'm airborne.

1082
00:39:16,480 --> 00:39:17,980
When it does that,
it says, this guy

1083
00:39:17,980 --> 00:39:20,240
isn't trying to dogfight
the airplane anymore.

1084
00:39:20,240 --> 00:39:21,530
They're trying to get gas.

1085
00:39:21,530 --> 00:39:24,023
So therefore I don't need
all the full roll capability

1086
00:39:24,023 --> 00:39:25,940
and everything else, and
really, what I really

1087
00:39:25,940 --> 00:39:27,470
want is an airplane
that's really

1088
00:39:27,470 --> 00:39:29,070
responsive with its lift.

1089
00:39:29,070 --> 00:39:32,720
And so, again, no input from
the pilot, but the leading edge

1090
00:39:32,720 --> 00:39:35,630
flaps will deploy down,
the trailing edge flap

1091
00:39:35,630 --> 00:39:37,400
and the ailerons will
also deploy down.

1092
00:39:37,400 --> 00:39:39,820
Again, they're trying to
create camber on that wing.

1093
00:39:39,820 --> 00:39:41,320
This is all digital
flight controls.

1094
00:39:41,320 --> 00:39:43,170
No input from the
pilot whatsoever.

1095
00:39:43,170 --> 00:39:45,170
PROFESSOR: Laz, what would
a typical airspeed be

1096
00:39:45,170 --> 00:39:47,390
when doing this air refueling?

1097
00:39:47,390 --> 00:39:49,400
RANDY GORDON: This is
about-- oops, sorry.

1098
00:39:49,400 --> 00:39:51,290
Let me go back one here.

1099
00:39:51,290 --> 00:39:56,000
This is about 300 knots or
so, something like that.

1100
00:39:56,000 --> 00:39:58,250
You've got to be able to
fly-- so for a Raptor, that's

1101
00:39:58,250 --> 00:39:59,630
kind of slow.

1102
00:39:59,630 --> 00:40:02,297
For the tanker airplane,
that's somewhat fast.

1103
00:40:02,297 --> 00:40:04,130
So you're trying to
find that marriage where

1104
00:40:04,130 --> 00:40:06,313
both airplanes have
good flying qualities

1105
00:40:06,313 --> 00:40:08,480
so that one's not about to
stall and the other one's

1106
00:40:08,480 --> 00:40:11,360
in full power trying
to catch up to him.

1107
00:40:11,360 --> 00:40:13,160
By the way, the
most unnatural act

1108
00:40:13,160 --> 00:40:14,870
I've ever done as
a fighter pilot

1109
00:40:14,870 --> 00:40:17,453
is to connect up my airplane
to another airplane.

1110
00:40:17,453 --> 00:40:18,620
I just never got used to it.

1111
00:40:18,620 --> 00:40:20,600
I can do it just fine,
but the whole notion

1112
00:40:20,600 --> 00:40:23,720
of this big boom connecting
my airplane to a big airliner

1113
00:40:23,720 --> 00:40:25,820
was just really strange
and weird to me.

1114
00:40:25,820 --> 00:40:28,570
Never got used to it.

1115
00:40:28,570 --> 00:40:29,070
OK.

1116
00:40:29,070 --> 00:40:30,010
Some of the limiters.

1117
00:40:30,010 --> 00:40:35,530
So in an F-15, because we talked
to that a little bit earlier,

1118
00:40:35,530 --> 00:40:37,678
you can get up to 500,
600 miles an hour.

1119
00:40:37,678 --> 00:40:39,220
You can take both
hands on the stick,

1120
00:40:39,220 --> 00:40:41,320
you can pull that
stick all the way back,

1121
00:40:41,320 --> 00:40:44,080
and you will rip the wings off
the airplane and you will turn

1122
00:40:44,080 --> 00:40:46,960
it into a big ball of metal
cascading down to the ground

1123
00:40:46,960 --> 00:40:50,230
because you have
over-g'ed the airplane.

1124
00:40:50,230 --> 00:40:52,660
Again, that awful, terrible
movie Top Gun in which

1125
00:40:52,660 --> 00:40:56,120
Tom Cruise departs the
airplane and gets into a spin,

1126
00:40:56,120 --> 00:40:58,900
again, you've exceeded some
capability of the airplane

1127
00:40:58,900 --> 00:41:00,700
and the airplane departs
controlled flight

1128
00:41:00,700 --> 00:41:03,310
and gets into some type
of uncontrolled situation.

1129
00:41:03,310 --> 00:41:06,670
On a Raptor, there used to be
in the pilot operating manual

1130
00:41:06,670 --> 00:41:09,610
a little blurb that said
quote you can maneuver

1131
00:41:09,610 --> 00:41:13,330
this airplane with
quote reckless abandon,

1132
00:41:13,330 --> 00:41:15,250
and you will not
over g the airplane,

1133
00:41:15,250 --> 00:41:17,710
you will not depart the
airplane from controlled flight.

1134
00:41:17,710 --> 00:41:22,385
They did a spectacular
job allowing this airplane

1135
00:41:22,385 --> 00:41:24,010
to get right to the
edge of performance

1136
00:41:24,010 --> 00:41:26,530
but not going over the top.

1137
00:41:26,530 --> 00:41:30,160
So one of the neat
things that they did--

1138
00:41:30,160 --> 00:41:31,840
airplane is in a left-hand turn.

1139
00:41:31,840 --> 00:41:32,830
Look at that aileron.

1140
00:41:32,830 --> 00:41:34,350
It's up.

1141
00:41:34,350 --> 00:41:35,640
Look at that aileron.

1142
00:41:35,640 --> 00:41:37,150
It's up.

1143
00:41:37,150 --> 00:41:40,460
What the crap is going on there?

1144
00:41:40,460 --> 00:41:41,960
And if you know
this one, you really

1145
00:41:41,960 --> 00:41:45,370
do get the model
because I didn't

1146
00:41:45,370 --> 00:41:47,862
know this for a long time.

1147
00:41:47,862 --> 00:41:49,570
So again, just for
sake of time because I

1148
00:41:49,570 --> 00:41:51,590
know we've got guys
coming in afterwards,

1149
00:41:51,590 --> 00:41:55,507
center of gravity of the
airplane sits right here.

1150
00:41:55,507 --> 00:41:57,090
In your engineering
classes you always

1151
00:41:57,090 --> 00:41:59,820
talk about forces acting
on the center of gravity.

1152
00:41:59,820 --> 00:42:02,070
And you take this whole
airplane and you model it down

1153
00:42:02,070 --> 00:42:05,430
to a point mass and
you say that's--

1154
00:42:05,430 --> 00:42:08,400
for sake of all the
sum of forces analysis.

1155
00:42:08,400 --> 00:42:13,260
Well, in real life this
airplane's about 43 feet wide.

1156
00:42:13,260 --> 00:42:15,340
Yeah, you can sum the
forces through there,

1157
00:42:15,340 --> 00:42:17,670
but there's forces acting
all over this airplane

1158
00:42:17,670 --> 00:42:19,020
at different spots.

1159
00:42:19,020 --> 00:42:21,180
Think about here on the wingtip.

1160
00:42:21,180 --> 00:42:23,100
I put g on the airplane.

1161
00:42:23,100 --> 00:42:25,320
And this wing tip, it's
almost like the wings want

1162
00:42:25,320 --> 00:42:27,900
to bend up as I'm coming down.

1163
00:42:27,900 --> 00:42:30,420
So you put a lot of stress
out on the wing tips.

1164
00:42:30,420 --> 00:42:32,940
So their answer,
quite ingeniously,

1165
00:42:32,940 --> 00:42:35,040
was to deflect the
ailerons down when

1166
00:42:35,040 --> 00:42:36,360
you're at high maneuvering.

1167
00:42:36,360 --> 00:42:37,590
The way you know you're
at high maneuvering

1168
00:42:37,590 --> 00:42:39,882
is you can kind of see these
little wispies forming off

1169
00:42:39,882 --> 00:42:40,560
the wingtips.

1170
00:42:40,560 --> 00:42:42,510
So you are creating
low pressure.

1171
00:42:42,510 --> 00:42:43,510
That's another one, too.

1172
00:42:43,510 --> 00:42:46,052
You can see the clouds forming
on the front edge of the wing.

1173
00:42:46,052 --> 00:42:48,510
Low pressure causes
that air to condense,

1174
00:42:48,510 --> 00:42:49,710
and so you make clouds.

1175
00:42:49,710 --> 00:42:52,050
So you know the airplane's
really maneuvering up.

1176
00:42:52,050 --> 00:42:55,020
And an answer to reduce
stress at the wingtips

1177
00:42:55,020 --> 00:42:56,950
was to deflect the ailerons up.

1178
00:42:56,950 --> 00:42:59,560
What happens to the lift out at
the wingtips with the ailerons

1179
00:42:59,560 --> 00:43:01,962
up?

1180
00:43:01,962 --> 00:43:02,670
Goes down, right?

1181
00:43:02,670 --> 00:43:04,740
So it helps to push
those wings back

1182
00:43:04,740 --> 00:43:07,920
down again so you're not trying
to overstress the airplane.

1183
00:43:07,920 --> 00:43:10,560
There are limiters like
this all over the airplane,

1184
00:43:10,560 --> 00:43:13,135
such that you won't
over g the airplane.

1185
00:43:13,135 --> 00:43:15,510
Literally, you can do anything
you want to this airplane.

1186
00:43:15,510 --> 00:43:18,420
That's what I'm telling you,
it's easier to fly a Raptor

1187
00:43:18,420 --> 00:43:20,790
than it is to fly a
Cessna because you really

1188
00:43:20,790 --> 00:43:22,380
have to pay attention to what
you're doing in a Cessna.

1189
00:43:22,380 --> 00:43:24,068
In a Raptor, I could
put my kid in there

1190
00:43:24,068 --> 00:43:26,610
and he can do this all day at
whatever speed, and nothing bad

1191
00:43:26,610 --> 00:43:27,777
will happen to the airplane.

1192
00:43:27,777 --> 00:43:31,485
It's really quite spectacular
how they did that.

1193
00:43:31,485 --> 00:43:32,860
The other part
I'll give you here

1194
00:43:32,860 --> 00:43:34,470
is some of the command systems.

1195
00:43:34,470 --> 00:43:37,970
So if I'm in a dogfight and I'm
trying to shoot the other guy,

1196
00:43:37,970 --> 00:43:41,160
I'm going against a
maneuvering target.

1197
00:43:41,160 --> 00:43:43,310
And so the flight
controls transfer over

1198
00:43:43,310 --> 00:43:45,950
to what's called a
g command system.

1199
00:43:45,950 --> 00:43:48,560
So if I'm maneuvering at 4
g's and I let go of the stick,

1200
00:43:48,560 --> 00:43:49,893
the airplane will stay at 4 g's.

1201
00:43:49,893 --> 00:43:52,102
And if I see that the target's
maneuvering and I need

1202
00:43:52,102 --> 00:43:53,967
to go to 6 g's or 7
g's, I'll put that in

1203
00:43:53,967 --> 00:43:55,300
and the airplane will keep that.

1204
00:43:55,300 --> 00:43:57,860
So in other words,
again, it's a vote.

1205
00:43:57,860 --> 00:43:59,760
I'm not physically
connected to anything.

1206
00:43:59,760 --> 00:44:02,240
I talk to the computer
and I say, I want 6 g's.

1207
00:44:02,240 --> 00:44:04,850
And the airplane does all types
of black magic and sorcery

1208
00:44:04,850 --> 00:44:08,210
behind me, and Lo and behold,
the airplane gets to 6 to 7

1209
00:44:08,210 --> 00:44:08,822
g's.

1210
00:44:08,822 --> 00:44:11,030
That really matters when
you're in a high maneuvering

1211
00:44:11,030 --> 00:44:13,910
kind of situation,
so it goes g command.

1212
00:44:13,910 --> 00:44:16,920
When you're slower,
i.e. on landing,

1213
00:44:16,920 --> 00:44:20,217
and I want to put that flight
path marker right on that edge

1214
00:44:20,217 --> 00:44:22,550
of the runway because that's
where I want to touch down,

1215
00:44:22,550 --> 00:44:26,700
right at the 1,000-foot
markers, then in that scenario,

1216
00:44:26,700 --> 00:44:28,850
I'm not really caring
so much about g command,

1217
00:44:28,850 --> 00:44:30,360
I care about pitch rate.

1218
00:44:30,360 --> 00:44:32,870
And so it transitions over
to a pitch rate command.

1219
00:44:32,870 --> 00:44:34,880
And if I put no
input on the stick,

1220
00:44:34,880 --> 00:44:38,240
it says zero pitch rate,
again, black magic and sorcery

1221
00:44:38,240 --> 00:44:39,170
will happen behind me.

1222
00:44:39,170 --> 00:44:40,280
The flight controls
will do whatever

1223
00:44:40,280 --> 00:44:42,800
they need to do to make sure
that my pitch rate stays

1224
00:44:42,800 --> 00:44:43,760
at zero.

1225
00:44:43,760 --> 00:44:46,100
So it transitions from one
type of light control system

1226
00:44:46,100 --> 00:44:46,642
to the other.

1227
00:44:46,642 --> 00:44:48,150
No pilot input whatsoever.

1228
00:44:48,150 --> 00:44:52,310
It's just based off of
flight command system.

1229
00:44:52,310 --> 00:44:52,810
All right.

1230
00:44:52,810 --> 00:44:55,268
So let's talk through a couple
of the implications of that,

1231
00:44:55,268 --> 00:44:58,320
and then we'll go to
questions at the end.

