[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

1299
00:47:18,814 --> 00:47:22,307
[MUSIC PLAYING]

1300
00:47:26,798 --> 00:47:29,090
[INAUDIBLE]

1301
00:47:29,090 --> 00:47:31,219
The horizontal stabilators
are streamlined.

1302
00:47:36,209 --> 00:47:42,200
[INAUDIBLE] Incidentally,
watch the flight controllers

1303
00:47:42,200 --> 00:47:43,844
in the back.

1304
00:47:43,844 --> 00:47:44,828
AUDIENCE: Oh, man.

1305
00:47:44,828 --> 00:47:46,200
We need somebody better here.

1306
00:47:46,200 --> 00:47:47,467
RANDY GORDON: I know, totally.

1307
00:47:47,467 --> 00:47:50,389
[LAUGHTER]

1308
00:47:53,320 --> 00:47:56,090
So at that speed, again,
it's the g command system.

1309
00:47:56,090 --> 00:47:57,950
The pilot is commanding
a certain g rate.

1310
00:47:57,950 --> 00:48:00,600
And miner deflections are
happening all the way here back

1311
00:48:00,600 --> 00:48:02,100
along the back edge
of the airplane.

1312
00:48:02,100 --> 00:48:04,040
The pilot has no input on that.

1313
00:48:04,040 --> 00:48:06,702
The system is doing everything
possible to command that g

1314
00:48:06,702 --> 00:48:07,910
that the pilot has asked for.

1315
00:48:10,372 --> 00:48:11,830
You'll see one
other maneuver here.

1316
00:48:11,830 --> 00:48:14,640
Pause for a second.

1317
00:48:14,640 --> 00:48:16,700
So what this maneuver is is--

1318
00:48:16,700 --> 00:48:19,610
and again, slow speed.

1319
00:48:19,610 --> 00:48:22,400
Airplane's going straight up,
and what they're trying to do

1320
00:48:22,400 --> 00:48:25,070
is basically pitch
forward completely and get

1321
00:48:25,070 --> 00:48:27,320
the airplane-- so it's almost
like you're flying an L,

1322
00:48:27,320 --> 00:48:29,040
like you're going
straight up and then you

1323
00:48:29,040 --> 00:48:30,915
want to pitch forward
and then accelerate out

1324
00:48:30,915 --> 00:48:32,318
horizontally that way.

1325
00:48:32,318 --> 00:48:34,610
The way gets that is through
that thrust vectoring that

1326
00:48:34,610 --> 00:48:35,167
happens.

1327
00:48:35,167 --> 00:48:37,250
But as that maneuver
happens, again, all the pilot

1328
00:48:37,250 --> 00:48:40,010
is doing is just doing a direct
push forward on the stick.

1329
00:48:40,010 --> 00:48:41,660
Watch what happens
to all the flight

1330
00:48:41,660 --> 00:48:43,455
controls in the
back of the airplane

1331
00:48:43,455 --> 00:48:45,830
to keep that airplane going
exactly where the pilot wants

1332
00:48:45,830 --> 00:48:46,160
it to.

1333
00:48:46,160 --> 00:48:46,660
Play.

1334
00:48:49,850 --> 00:48:51,470
So you'll see huge
deflections out

1335
00:48:51,470 --> 00:48:53,120
of that horizontal stabilizer.

1336
00:48:53,120 --> 00:48:53,870
It's kind of neat.

1337
00:48:53,870 --> 00:48:54,745
You can keep playing.

1338
00:49:09,110 --> 00:49:11,400
So what you're seeing
there is, from the outside

1339
00:49:11,400 --> 00:49:13,560
of the airplane-- we'll
see this more on the demo--

1340
00:49:13,560 --> 00:49:16,933
is the airplane is essentially
pirouetting in the sky.

1341
00:49:16,933 --> 00:49:19,350
So it's falling straight down,
but it's very controllable.

1342
00:49:19,350 --> 00:49:23,073
It's flying at speeds about
60 to 65 miles an hour,

1343
00:49:23,073 --> 00:49:24,240
but very, very controllable.

1344
00:49:24,240 --> 00:49:26,498
And you'll see every bit
of flight control surface

1345
00:49:26,498 --> 00:49:28,290
on the back end of this
airplane deflecting

1346
00:49:28,290 --> 00:49:29,620
to do what it needs to do.

1347
00:49:29,620 --> 00:49:32,910
So don't think of it in
terms of aileron, elevator.

1348
00:49:32,910 --> 00:49:36,270
It's a little bit fluid when
it comes to a Raptor's stuff.

1349
00:49:36,270 --> 00:49:37,320
Stuff happens back there.

1350
00:49:37,320 --> 00:49:38,280
PROFESSOR: Touch and go video?

1351
00:49:38,280 --> 00:49:40,490
RANDY GORDON: OK, so this
shows a little bit of-- yeah,

1352
00:49:40,490 --> 00:49:41,782
you can go ahead and play that.

1353
00:49:46,500 --> 00:49:47,740
And hit pause for a second.

1354
00:49:47,740 --> 00:49:50,310
We'll just do the setup.

1355
00:49:50,310 --> 00:49:51,090
OK.

1356
00:49:51,090 --> 00:49:53,640
So very quick, the setup
for this video, this

1357
00:49:53,640 --> 00:49:56,850
is the downside of a digital
flight control system.

1358
00:49:56,850 --> 00:49:59,700
Because on this
airplane, again, it's

1359
00:49:59,700 --> 00:50:02,640
pure cables, pulleys,
it's ratios and gears

1360
00:50:02,640 --> 00:50:04,520
and stuff like that.

1361
00:50:04,520 --> 00:50:07,050
On this airplane,
it's software code.

1362
00:50:07,050 --> 00:50:10,050
It's zeros and ones, and
you better get it right.

1363
00:50:10,050 --> 00:50:11,880
And everything is
interconnected.

1364
00:50:11,880 --> 00:50:13,973
We talked about how
moving the gear handle

1365
00:50:13,973 --> 00:50:16,140
tells the flight control
system something different.

1366
00:50:16,140 --> 00:50:17,940
If I open the air
refueling door,

1367
00:50:17,940 --> 00:50:20,430
it tells the flight control
something different.

1368
00:50:20,430 --> 00:50:24,000
In this case, the power
setting of the airplane

1369
00:50:24,000 --> 00:50:27,750
tells the airplane
something different.

1370
00:50:27,750 --> 00:50:30,420
One of my good friends,
test pilot, outstanding guy.

1371
00:50:30,420 --> 00:50:32,230
So don't think of this
as he's a bad pilot.

1372
00:50:32,230 --> 00:50:34,300
He's not, he's awesome.

1373
00:50:34,300 --> 00:50:36,990
But the jet believes
something that

1374
00:50:36,990 --> 00:50:39,390
wasn't really true because
there was an error in how

1375
00:50:39,390 --> 00:50:41,130
the software was coded.

1376
00:50:41,130 --> 00:50:43,950
And you'll see the
first approach--

1377
00:50:43,950 --> 00:50:46,210
he'll take off again on
what's called military power,

1378
00:50:46,210 --> 00:50:48,030
so they're not
using afterburner,

1379
00:50:48,030 --> 00:50:50,610
and the airplane
behaves just fine.

1380
00:50:50,610 --> 00:50:53,630
The next time around, he goes
around using afterburner power,

1381
00:50:53,630 --> 00:50:56,130
so you'll see fire come out of
the back end of the airplane.

1382
00:50:56,130 --> 00:50:58,860
And that changes something
in the flight system

1383
00:50:58,860 --> 00:51:01,410
for the engines, which
tells the flight control

1384
00:51:01,410 --> 00:51:03,570
computer a different condition.

1385
00:51:03,570 --> 00:51:06,580
And what ended up happening is
that the gains, if you will,

1386
00:51:06,580 --> 00:51:09,422
of the stick were
completely off.

1387
00:51:09,422 --> 00:51:11,880
So again, this would be like
if you were on a highway speed

1388
00:51:11,880 --> 00:51:13,547
and that same wheel
deflection you would

1389
00:51:13,547 --> 00:51:15,480
use to park your car
in your garage, now

1390
00:51:15,480 --> 00:51:17,280
that little deflection
of your wheel

1391
00:51:17,280 --> 00:51:19,710
makes that same turn
of the tires up front.

1392
00:51:19,710 --> 00:51:24,062
So you get into what's called a
pilot-induced oscillation, PIO,

1393
00:51:24,062 --> 00:51:26,520
which basically says you're
out of phase with the airplane.

1394
00:51:26,520 --> 00:51:28,937
If I'm driving my car and I
turn the steering wheel right,

1395
00:51:28,937 --> 00:51:30,090
the car is going left.

1396
00:51:30,090 --> 00:51:32,010
As it's going left, I'm
trying to correct, I go right,

1397
00:51:32,010 --> 00:51:33,240
and now the thing goes right.

1398
00:51:33,240 --> 00:51:35,280
And so you get out of
phase with the airplane,

1399
00:51:35,280 --> 00:51:36,447
and you'll see what happens.

1400
00:51:36,447 --> 00:51:37,440
Go ahead and play.

1401
00:51:37,440 --> 00:51:38,970
By the way, you see all
the flight control surfaces

1402
00:51:38,970 --> 00:51:40,200
deflecting in the back?

1403
00:51:40,200 --> 00:51:41,860
This is now in a
pitch rate system.

1404
00:51:41,860 --> 00:51:43,568
So he's just trying
to land the airplane.

1405
00:51:43,568 --> 00:51:45,152
The back of the
airplane does whatever

1406
00:51:45,152 --> 00:51:47,550
it needs to do to keep that
flight path marker exactly

1407
00:51:47,550 --> 00:51:48,425
where it needs to go.

1408
00:51:52,658 --> 00:51:54,950
And again, all the flight
control surface is deflected.

1409
00:51:54,950 --> 00:51:57,650
Military power because the
engines are black, if you will,

1410
00:51:57,650 --> 00:51:58,322
on this one.

1411
00:51:58,322 --> 00:51:59,780
On the next time
around, you'll see

1412
00:51:59,780 --> 00:52:02,630
him maneuver with
afterburner, and you'll

1413
00:52:02,630 --> 00:52:05,026
see him get into this
pitch-induced oscillation.

1414
00:52:13,217 --> 00:52:14,800
That's the chase
airplane, by the way.

1415
00:52:14,800 --> 00:52:16,470
It's an F-16 that
follows them around.

1416
00:52:24,300 --> 00:52:26,030
So same thing, getting
set up to land.

1417
00:52:26,030 --> 00:52:28,100
The camera goes out of
focus here for a second,

1418
00:52:28,100 --> 00:52:29,240
comes back in.

1419
00:52:29,240 --> 00:52:30,740
Again, just notice
everything that's

1420
00:52:30,740 --> 00:52:32,620
happening on the back
end of this airplane

1421
00:52:32,620 --> 00:52:35,120
to keep that pitch rate where
the pilot has commanded it to.

1422
00:52:39,953 --> 00:52:41,120
Two things will happen here.

1423
00:52:41,120 --> 00:52:43,037
He selects afterburner
and he raises the gear.

1424
00:52:43,037 --> 00:52:45,050
That changes the
flight control laws.

1425
00:52:45,050 --> 00:52:48,290
And the gains were
not set correctly.

1426
00:52:48,290 --> 00:52:49,290
There's the afterburner.

1427
00:52:49,290 --> 00:52:52,902
And now you see where he's out
of phase with the airplane.

1428
00:52:52,902 --> 00:52:55,360
And he's doing everything to
keep the airplane from hitting

1429
00:52:55,360 --> 00:52:57,130
the ground, and can't avoid it.

1430
00:52:59,670 --> 00:53:04,500
He's OK, but the airplane
was fairly well scraped,

1431
00:53:04,500 --> 00:53:05,920
as you can imagine.

1432
00:53:05,920 --> 00:53:07,350
So it's just a danger.

1433
00:53:07,350 --> 00:53:09,270
The digital flight
controls allow a lot

1434
00:53:09,270 --> 00:53:11,370
of flexibility and creativity.

1435
00:53:11,370 --> 00:53:13,950
There used to be a term,
eh, it's only software,

1436
00:53:13,950 --> 00:53:15,045
we can figure it out.

1437
00:53:15,045 --> 00:53:19,860
Not true when you're dealing
with vehicles like this,

1438
00:53:19,860 --> 00:53:21,507
where small changes
in software code

1439
00:53:21,507 --> 00:53:24,090
can have dramatic implications
on the ability of the airplane.

1440
00:53:24,090 --> 00:53:27,143
So it takes an
amazing amount of--

1441
00:53:27,143 --> 00:53:27,810
PROFESSOR: Demo?

1442
00:53:27,810 --> 00:53:29,070
RANDY GORDON: Yeah, you can
