1474
00:54:29,990 --> 00:54:32,150
well, one, the question was
the thrust to weight ratio.

1475
00:54:32,150 --> 00:54:34,275
It's greater than one to
one-- a little bit greater

1476
00:54:34,275 --> 00:54:35,900
than one to one on takeoff.

1477
00:54:35,900 --> 00:54:38,070
By the way, watch this
maneuver here real quick.

1478
00:54:38,070 --> 00:54:40,757
So that's the thrust
vectoring kicking

1479
00:54:40,757 --> 00:54:43,340
in to really get the airplane--
so initially it has the flight

1480
00:54:43,340 --> 00:54:44,167
control surfaces.

1481
00:54:44,167 --> 00:54:46,250
And then as things slow
down, the thrust vectoring

1482
00:54:46,250 --> 00:54:50,990
kicks in to basically turn the
airplane into a flat plate.

1483
00:54:50,990 --> 00:54:53,835
Second question was about the
thrust vectoring on takeoff.

1484
00:54:53,835 --> 00:54:56,460
So it'll put it a little bit up,
not much, just a little bit up

1485
00:54:56,460 --> 00:55:00,540
to help the nose
rotate just a tad.

1486
00:55:00,540 --> 00:55:03,220
This is that high angle of
attack maneuver, if you will.

1487
00:55:07,580 --> 00:55:08,080
Keep going.

1488
00:55:08,080 --> 00:55:10,090
It's all good questions.

1489
00:55:10,090 --> 00:55:10,590
Yeah?

1490
00:55:10,590 --> 00:55:12,923
AUDIENCE: You said all the
software is written basically

1491
00:55:12,923 --> 00:55:14,750
so the plane can't
damage itself.

1492
00:55:14,750 --> 00:55:16,110
Does the pilot become a limiter?

1493
00:55:16,110 --> 00:55:18,555
Can it put you in situations
where it can hurt you?

1494
00:55:18,555 --> 00:55:19,930
RANDY GORDON:
Yeah, very much so.

1495
00:55:19,930 --> 00:55:22,050
In fact, we've kind of
achieved that spot now,

1496
00:55:22,050 --> 00:55:23,852
where the pilot very
much is limiting

1497
00:55:23,852 --> 00:55:26,310
the performance of the airplane
itself because the airplane

1498
00:55:26,310 --> 00:55:28,540
could do so much more.

1499
00:55:28,540 --> 00:55:31,110
So this is what's called a
fifth generation fighter.

1500
00:55:31,110 --> 00:55:35,182
The first generation was like
an old Korean War, like F-86

1501
00:55:35,182 --> 00:55:35,890
kind of airplane.

1502
00:55:35,890 --> 00:55:38,130
And then successively
through the generations,

1503
00:55:38,130 --> 00:55:39,720
you arrive at this
fifth generation,

1504
00:55:39,720 --> 00:55:41,760
which to me is the
pinnacle of what

1505
00:55:41,760 --> 00:55:45,480
you could get with a human
and an airplane together.

1506
00:55:45,480 --> 00:55:48,840
Sixth generation is going to
involve teaming this airplane

1507
00:55:48,840 --> 00:55:51,913
with unmanned airplanes,
and the unmanned airplanes

1508
00:55:51,913 --> 00:55:54,330
are going to have a lot more
capability from a maneuvering

1509
00:55:54,330 --> 00:55:55,747
standpoint because
they don't have

1510
00:55:55,747 --> 00:55:57,580
the limitation of the
pilot and all the life

1511
00:55:57,580 --> 00:55:59,831
support systems that come
with it and everything else.

1512
00:55:59,831 --> 00:56:02,130
So all that weight you would
normally devote to that,

1513
00:56:02,130 --> 00:56:04,308
you can get away with
putting other stuff in there.

1514
00:56:04,308 --> 00:56:06,600
When I was telling you it
flies at about the same speed

1515
00:56:06,600 --> 00:56:08,203
as a Cessna--

1516
00:56:08,203 --> 00:56:09,870
I mean, this is those
types of maneuvers

1517
00:56:09,870 --> 00:56:10,890
there where you can
get away with that.

1518
00:56:10,890 --> 00:56:13,057
Again, watch the flight
control surfaces in the back

1519
00:56:13,057 --> 00:56:15,950
and what's going on.

1520
00:56:15,950 --> 00:56:18,350
Later on they'll do a pass
where he'll open up the doors

1521
00:56:18,350 --> 00:56:19,940
and you can see the main
weapon bay, which is

1522
00:56:19,940 --> 00:56:21,230
underneath the airplane belly.

1523
00:56:21,230 --> 00:56:23,390
And then you'll see the
side weapon bay doors,

1524
00:56:23,390 --> 00:56:25,390
so you can kind of see
where all the weapons are

1525
00:56:25,390 --> 00:56:26,660
carried inside the airplane.

1526
00:56:26,660 --> 00:56:29,070
Again, a huge center
of gravity challenge.

1527
00:56:29,070 --> 00:56:30,320
So here's that maneuver there.

1528
00:56:30,320 --> 00:56:31,445
You can see the doors open.

1529
00:56:34,223 --> 00:56:35,890
One thing I didn't
really tell you about

1530
00:56:35,890 --> 00:56:38,257
is, so in that such
situation, because missiles

1531
00:56:38,257 --> 00:56:40,840
weigh a couple hundred pounds a
piece or so, some of the bombs

1532
00:56:40,840 --> 00:56:43,120
are about 1,000 pounds apiece,
when you lose all that weight

1533
00:56:43,120 --> 00:56:44,828
immediately, it's
literally like dropping

1534
00:56:44,828 --> 00:56:46,990
a car off the front
end of your airplane.

1535
00:56:46,990 --> 00:56:49,210
The way that it fixes that
center of gravity issue

1536
00:56:49,210 --> 00:56:51,380
is by changing fuel
inside the airplane.

1537
00:56:51,380 --> 00:56:53,470
So it sloshes fuel
forward or back

1538
00:56:53,470 --> 00:56:54,910
to keep the center of gravity.

1539
00:56:54,910 --> 00:56:57,317
Again, there's no
fuel control panel

1540
00:56:57,317 --> 00:56:59,650
where I go, well, move this
and click this and whatever.

1541
00:56:59,650 --> 00:57:01,317
It does it all
completely automatically.

1542
00:57:01,317 --> 00:57:02,024
Go ahead.

1543
00:57:02,024 --> 00:57:03,607
AUDIENCE: What kind
of sensors can you

1544
00:57:03,607 --> 00:57:05,838
use to detect the
center of gravity?

1545
00:57:05,838 --> 00:57:07,630
RANDY GORDON: The
sensors that you can use?

1546
00:57:07,630 --> 00:57:10,810
So all of the fuel
tanks are instrumented,

1547
00:57:10,810 --> 00:57:12,820
so you know the status
of fuel in terms

1548
00:57:12,820 --> 00:57:15,070
of where things are set up.

1549
00:57:15,070 --> 00:57:17,228
You tell it-- well,
actually, it has the ability

1550
00:57:17,228 --> 00:57:18,520
to know what's on the airplane.

1551
00:57:18,520 --> 00:57:20,020
So when you load a
missile, it goes,

1552
00:57:20,020 --> 00:57:22,487
oh, it's this type of missile
and it weighs this much,

1553
00:57:22,487 --> 00:57:24,070
and it knows the
mass properties of it

1554
00:57:24,070 --> 00:57:26,948
and so it sets it up from there.

1555
00:57:26,948 --> 00:57:28,350
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

1556
00:57:28,350 --> 00:57:32,220
RANDY GORDON: So it actually
identifies that all by itself.

1557
00:57:32,220 --> 00:57:35,210
So like in a Cirrus, you
have to actually pull up

1558
00:57:35,210 --> 00:57:37,610
a screen in the Cirrus and
go, my passenger weighs this

1559
00:57:37,610 --> 00:57:39,340
and I've got this
baggage on board,

1560
00:57:39,340 --> 00:57:41,090
and then it gives you
the picture of where

1561
00:57:41,090 --> 00:57:42,308
the center of gravity is.

1562
00:57:42,308 --> 00:57:43,850
This thing, because
it's all digital,

1563
00:57:43,850 --> 00:57:46,142
the missile has a little
connector rod that connects it

1564
00:57:46,142 --> 00:57:48,040
and it says, behold,
I'm a missile.

1565
00:57:48,040 --> 00:57:49,180
AUDIENCE: What about
the pilot weight?

1566
00:57:49,180 --> 00:57:50,638
RANDY GORDON: Oh,
the pilot weight?

1567
00:57:50,638 --> 00:57:53,900
Doesn't matter because
they spec'ed it--

1568
00:57:53,900 --> 00:57:56,900
well, it matters but
for a different reason.

1569
00:57:56,900 --> 00:57:59,330
It matters for the ejection
seat, predominantly.

1570
00:57:59,330 --> 00:58:01,520
Because the ejection seat--

1571
00:58:01,520 --> 00:58:04,190
I think it's like 135
pounds is the lightest,

1572
00:58:04,190 --> 00:58:08,420
something like that, 115 to
135 to, like, 220 or something.

1573
00:58:08,420 --> 00:58:10,120
It's something around that spot.

1574
00:58:10,120 --> 00:58:12,620
So to be able to be within the
safe envelope of the ejection

1575
00:58:12,620 --> 00:58:16,430
seat, that's where the
weight of the pilot matters.

1576
00:58:16,430 --> 00:58:18,800
But they spec the center
of gravity such that

1577
00:58:18,800 --> 00:58:21,325
so long as really
anyone can sit up front

1578
00:58:21,325 --> 00:58:23,700
and you're not going to throw
off the CG of the airplane,

1579
00:58:23,700 --> 00:58:26,300
even though you're way
far forward of the CG

1580
00:58:26,300 --> 00:58:28,018
and there's a moment arm there.

1581
00:58:28,018 --> 00:58:29,810
But between everything
else in the cockpit,

1582
00:58:29,810 --> 00:58:32,240
that ejection seat is
ridiculously heavy.

1583
00:58:32,240 --> 00:58:34,903
And all the avionics that sit
up front, the radars up front,

1584
00:58:34,903 --> 00:58:36,320
all these things
that sit up there

1585
00:58:36,320 --> 00:58:38,653
have far more of a contribution
to the center of gravity

1586
00:58:38,653 --> 00:58:42,380
than you do, unless you're,
like, Shaq or something

1587
00:58:42,380 --> 00:58:42,880
like that.

1588
00:58:42,880 --> 00:58:43,910
But go ahead.

1589
00:58:43,910 --> 00:58:49,790
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]
on the helmet screen?

1590
00:58:49,790 --> 00:58:51,918
RANDY GORDON: So in
this airplane, not yet.

1591
00:58:51,918 --> 00:58:52,960
That's coming along soon.

1592
00:58:52,960 --> 00:58:55,377
Some of the older airplanes,
believe it or not, have that.

1593
00:58:55,377 --> 00:58:58,330
Like the F-15 that I flew,
you had a special helmet

1594
00:58:58,330 --> 00:59:02,418
that had all of the information
displayed on the visor itself.

1595
00:59:02,418 --> 00:59:04,960
So in addition to that hands-on
throttle and stick so I don't

1596
00:59:04,960 --> 00:59:06,502
have to take my
hands off of anything

1597
00:59:06,502 --> 00:59:08,170
to touch anything
in the cockpit,

1598
00:59:08,170 --> 00:59:10,090
I also don't have to
look in the cockpit

1599
00:59:10,090 --> 00:59:13,470
to see altitude,
air speed, heading.

1600
00:59:13,470 --> 00:59:16,600
It's all displayed
to me upfront.

1601
00:59:16,600 --> 00:59:19,700
This airplane eventually
will get that.

1602
00:59:19,700 --> 00:59:22,120
This is a programmatic
discussion now.

1603
00:59:22,120 --> 00:59:24,370
They only had so many dollars
to spend, and they said,

1604
00:59:24,370 --> 00:59:26,830
this airplane is so
awesome that it shouldn't

1605
00:59:26,830 --> 00:59:29,080
need a helmet-mounted display
system because it should

1606
00:59:29,080 --> 00:59:31,930
be able to see all the bad
guys from far enough away

1607
00:59:31,930 --> 00:59:33,460
and not be a problem.

1608
00:59:33,460 --> 00:59:36,172
We fielded the
airplane that way.

1609
00:59:36,172 --> 00:59:37,630
The first batch of
pilots, they all

1610
00:59:37,630 --> 00:59:41,210
transitioned from airplanes that
had that helmet-mounted system.

1611
00:59:41,210 --> 00:59:43,510
And they came to this airplane
where it didn't have it,

1612
00:59:43,510 --> 00:59:44,540
and they were pissed.

1613
00:59:44,540 --> 00:59:46,600
They were like, we've
got to have that back.

1614
00:59:46,600 --> 00:59:48,433
So that's kind of where
a lot of the efforts

1615
00:59:48,433 --> 00:59:50,910
now are going to modernize
the airplane a bit.

1616
00:59:50,910 --> 00:59:51,420
What else?

1617
00:59:51,420 --> 00:59:52,045
Good questions.

1618
00:59:52,045 --> 00:59:52,630
Go ahead.

1619
00:59:52,630 --> 00:59:53,207
Yeah?

1620
00:59:53,207 --> 00:59:55,540
AUDIENCE: Why is there a need
for humans in the cockpit?

1621
00:59:55,540 --> 00:59:57,810
Why not just go for
unmanned systems?

1622
00:59:57,810 --> 01:00:01,760
RANDY GORDON: Yeah, it is the
great debate of the fighter

1623
01:00:01,760 --> 01:00:02,787
community right now.

1624
01:00:02,787 --> 01:00:05,120
You're really touching on
something really, really deep.

1625
01:00:07,800 --> 01:00:10,933
The best answer so far
is that the greatest--

1626
01:00:10,933 --> 01:00:13,350
I mean, we talked a lot about
the hardware and the systems

1627
01:00:13,350 --> 01:00:14,310
on board the airplane.

1628
01:00:14,310 --> 01:00:16,710
Really, the greatest piece
of capability on the airplane

1629
01:00:16,710 --> 01:00:20,990
is the mind of the
person flying up front.

1630
01:00:20,990 --> 01:00:24,120
If it's a very dynamic
and changing environment,

1631
01:00:24,120 --> 01:00:26,820
to be able to tell a machine
to be able to incorporate all

1632
01:00:26,820 --> 01:00:30,090
those inputs and make the
right decisions based on that,

1633
01:00:30,090 --> 01:00:31,350
kind of hard to do right now.

1634
01:00:31,350 --> 01:00:33,850
I'm not saying we're not going
to get there, just right now,

1635
01:00:33,850 --> 01:00:34,790
it's difficult.

1636
01:00:34,790 --> 01:00:36,510
Where we use unmanned
systems a lot

1637
01:00:36,510 --> 01:00:39,080
now is in surveillance
missions, where

1638
01:00:39,080 --> 01:00:40,330
you can just launch the thing.

1639
01:00:40,330 --> 01:00:42,090
And it's got a
pre-programmed navigation,

1640
01:00:42,090 --> 01:00:44,940
and it knows what it needs to
do and it can set things up.

1641
01:00:44,940 --> 01:00:47,340
Those are somewhat
bounded problems,

1642
01:00:47,340 --> 01:00:49,200
is probably the best
way to describe it,

1643
01:00:49,200 --> 01:00:51,450
where you could use an
unmanned system for that.

1644
01:00:51,450 --> 01:00:53,790
Where it gets difficulties
is in combat situation,

1645
01:00:53,790 --> 01:00:55,230
it's extremely dynamic.

1646
01:00:55,230 --> 01:00:59,100
It's battle royale,
WWF, mosh pit,

1647
01:00:59,100 --> 01:01:01,110
any possible chaotic
situation you can put,

1648
01:01:01,110 --> 01:01:03,130
that's kind of
what it looks like.

1649
01:01:03,130 --> 01:01:05,700
And so having a human mind
attached in that environment,

1650
01:01:05,700 --> 01:01:07,993
to be able to adapt and do
what the mind does better

1651
01:01:07,993 --> 01:01:09,660
than a machine does,
at least right now,

1652
01:01:09,660 --> 01:01:15,600
that's the main argument to keep
humans in the system for now.

1653
01:01:15,600 --> 01:01:16,830
Yeah?

1654
01:01:16,830 --> 01:01:19,540
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] operate
these planes [INAUDIBLE]

1655
01:01:19,540 --> 01:01:23,630
to remotely operate
them, is the [INAUDIBLE]??

1656
01:01:23,630 --> 01:01:25,176
RANDY GORDON: It is.

1657
01:01:25,176 --> 01:01:26,930
PROFESSOR: Can you
repeat the question?

1658
01:01:26,930 --> 01:01:29,472
RANDY GORDON: Yeah, the question
is that if you operate these

1659
01:01:29,472 --> 01:01:32,180
things remotely--
so even if you had--

1660
01:01:32,180 --> 01:01:35,170
classic example, we'll
use Sully Sullenberger

1661
01:01:35,170 --> 01:01:37,760
because we talked about
Sully in the previous one.

1662
01:01:37,760 --> 01:01:40,280
You take off out of LaGuardia,
you lose both engines.

1663
01:01:40,280 --> 01:01:43,550
If you could have a remote pilot
sitting somewhere else that

1664
01:01:43,550 --> 01:01:45,590
could take over and
decide land on the Hudson,

1665
01:01:45,590 --> 01:01:48,850
because a machine most likely
wouldn't have made that choice,

1666
01:01:48,850 --> 01:01:50,390
is there a time lag issue?

1667
01:01:50,390 --> 01:01:52,430
The answer is 100% yes.

1668
01:01:52,430 --> 01:01:54,450
There is a time lag
issue between getting

1669
01:01:54,450 --> 01:01:56,450
that information displayed
down, make the input,

1670
01:01:56,450 --> 01:01:59,450
that input goes back
up and comes back over.

1671
01:01:59,450 --> 01:02:02,930
A thing we've done at
test pilot school is--

1672
01:02:02,930 --> 01:02:05,870
remember we showed earlier
that flight path marker,

1673
01:02:05,870 --> 01:02:07,862
where as a pilot you put
the flight path marker

1674
01:02:07,862 --> 01:02:10,070
and that's exactly where
your airplane's going to go?

1675
01:02:10,070 --> 01:02:12,560
If there's a time lag, that
will actually mess you up

1676
01:02:12,560 --> 01:02:14,930
because there's some second
or two delay between what

1677
01:02:14,930 --> 01:02:16,920
you're seeing and what the
airplane's actually going.

1678
01:02:16,920 --> 01:02:18,378
At test pilot
school, they actually

1679
01:02:18,378 --> 01:02:22,822
built a flight path marker that
accounts for that time delay.

1680
01:02:22,822 --> 01:02:25,280
If it knows that time delay,
it will actually count for it.

1681
01:02:25,280 --> 01:02:29,330
So you could fly the airplane
remotely even with the time lag

1682
01:02:29,330 --> 01:02:31,683
and still be able to do very
high gain tasks, like land

1683
01:02:31,683 --> 01:02:33,350
the airplane, for
instance, where things

1684
01:02:33,350 --> 01:02:34,460
are changing very rapidly.

1685
01:02:34,460 --> 01:02:37,140
So it's a new science, not
fully fleshed out here.

1686
01:02:37,140 --> 01:02:38,720
But the proof of
concept has been

1687
01:02:38,720 --> 01:02:42,920
demonstrated, to control
an airplane remotely even

1688
01:02:42,920 --> 01:02:44,390
with the time lag.

1689
01:02:44,390 --> 01:02:46,040
Who really cares
about that right now

1690
01:02:46,040 --> 01:02:49,178
are the airlines because,
to be very frank,

1691
01:02:49,178 --> 01:02:51,470
the pilots tend to be a pain
in the butt for an airline

1692
01:02:51,470 --> 01:02:52,462
company.

1693
01:02:52,462 --> 01:02:54,170
And if you can remove
the pilots and just

1694
01:02:54,170 --> 01:02:57,860
have a remote operated system
or even an autonomous system,

1695
01:02:57,860 --> 01:02:59,990
from a business
standpoint, the company

1696
01:02:59,990 --> 01:03:01,960
really likes that from
an aviation standpoint.

1697
01:03:01,960 --> 01:03:03,860
It triggers all
types of discussions

1698
01:03:03,860 --> 01:03:06,480
in a lot of other issues,
but that's one question.

1699
01:03:06,480 --> 01:03:06,980
Go ahead.

1700
01:03:06,980 --> 01:03:09,290
PROFESSOR: Let me jump in
for a minute with that.

1701
01:03:09,290 --> 01:03:13,310
I think actually to me, one of
the holy grails of GA safety

1702
01:03:13,310 --> 01:03:19,430
would be to have a
human co-pilot, perhaps,

1703
01:03:19,430 --> 01:03:20,570
on the ground.

1704
01:03:20,570 --> 01:03:22,070
So if you had that
kind of telemetry

1705
01:03:22,070 --> 01:03:26,858
that you have in a drone, a
human somewhere else could say,

1706
01:03:26,858 --> 01:03:28,400
you're running a
little short on fuel

1707
01:03:28,400 --> 01:03:30,275
or you forgot to change
tanks, all the things

1708
01:03:30,275 --> 01:03:33,530
that a human co-pilot could
do right in the cockpit.

1709
01:03:33,530 --> 01:03:35,270
Most of that safety
running checklist

